🐛 Describe the bug
Consider the program below:
import torch

d = {}

@torch.compile(backend="eager", fullgraph=True)
def fn(x, key):
    if key not in d:
        d[key] = 1
    return x.sin()

x = torch.randn(2)
fn(x, "torch")

with torch.compiler.set_stance("fail_on_recompile"):
    fn(x, "torch")
This code uses a common lazy-initialization pattern found in real-world libraries (e.g. einops). However, under torch.compile, it triggers a recompilation on the second execution due to the global dict mutation.
For einops this can be "fixed" by initializing the library beforehand, but it would be good if we could have a way to prevent this from happening.
Error logs
No response
Versions
pytorch main
cc @chauhang @penguinwu @voznesenskym @EikanWang @jgong5 @Guobing-Chen @XiaobingSuper @zhuhaozhe @blzheng @wenzhe-nrv @jiayisunx @kadeng @amjames @Lucaskabela @jataylo

Background and the problem
We are adding and improving PT2E-based float8 quantization on X86 CPU. And we still plan to get peak performance via Inductor CPP backend. Quantized float8 models will contain data type conversions from fp32 to fp8_e4m3 during quantization of input tensors.
In the generated cpp code of Inductor, the conversion is done by at::vec::convert, whose current implementation is based on the AVX512 ISA. And it is a reference implementation with a lot of bitwise operations since the AVX512 ISA does not contain any instructions that convert fp32 to fp8. So, it's performance won't be peak.
Now the new AVX10.2 ISA provides a set of instructions to convert fp16 to fp8. Therefore, the fp32 -> fp8 conversion can be done with two instructions (fp32 -> fp16 -> fp8), which is apparently much faster than the reference implementation. We definitely want to use this new instruction, if available, in the Inductor CPP backend for peak performance.
Since ATen's dynamic dispatch does not support ISA newer than AVX512, it won't be feasible to add a new version of at::vec::convert with dynamic dispatch. We need to use the new instruction without dynamic dispatch in Inductor's CPP backend.
Proposals
at::vec::convert for dtype conversion is used during the codegen of Inductor. So, we can either modify at::vec::convert or use another function in the codegen. Either way, the new implementation will only take effect with gcc >= 15 on a platform with AVX10.2 support and won't break the dynamic dispatch mechanism in any cases.
Option 1: add AVX10.2 code in at::vec::convert with a __AVX10_2__ macro guard
Changes:
diff --git a/aten/src/ATen/cpu/vec/vec512/vec512_float8.h b/aten/src/ATen/cpu/vec/vec512/vec512_float8.h
index 0a54986d82b..261cb7506b6 100644
--- a/aten/src/ATen/cpu/vec/vec512/vec512_float8.h
+++ b/aten/src/ATen/cpu/vec/vec512/vec512_float8.h
@@ -95,6 +95,12 @@ static inline void cvtfp8e4m3_fp32(const __m128i& a, __m512& o) {
 }

 static inline __m128i cvtfp32_fp8e4m3(const __m512& src) {
+#ifdef __AVX10_2__
+  __m256i f16_vec = _mm512_cvt_roundps_ph(
+      src,
+      _MM_FROUND_TO_NEAREST_INT |_MM_FROUND_NO_EXC);
+  return _mm256_cvtph_hf8(_mm256_castsi256_ph(f16_vec));
+#else
   // cvt 16x32 from fp32 to fp8 e4m3
   const __m512i sign_mask = _mm512_set1_epi32(0x80000000);
   const __m512i fp8_max = _mm512_set1_epi32(UINT32_C(1087) << 20);
@@ -156,6 +162,7 @@ static inline __m128i cvtfp32_fp8e4m3(const __m512& src) {

   // Narrow 32-bit integers to 8-bit
   return _mm512_cvtepi32_epi8(packed);
+#endif
 }

 static inline float fp8e4m3_to_fp32_scalar(uint8_t val) {
Pros:

Least changes of code

Cons:

Introduces newer ISA than AVX512 to aten code, which might be undesired.

Option 2: Add a new function with both the reference and AVX10.2 implementations in Inductor's cpp_prefix.h
Changes:
diff --git a/torch/_inductor/codegen/cpp.py b/torch/_inductor/codegen/cpp.py
index 7706423e09a..40167f326d4 100644
--- a/torch/_inductor/codegen/cpp.py
+++ b/torch/_inductor/codegen/cpp.py
@@ -3502,7 +3502,10 @@ class CppVecKernel(CppKernel):
             expr = f"{src}.to<{dst_cpp_type},{dst_num_vectors}>()"
         elif src_dtype != dtype:
             if src_num_vectors == dst_num_vectors == 1:
-                expr = f"at::vec::convert<{dst_cpp_type}>({src})"
+                if src_dtype == torch.float and dtype == torch.float8_e4m3fn:
+                    expr = f"convert_float32_float8_e4m3fn({src})"  # defined in cpp_prefix.h with AVX10.2
+                else:
+                    expr = f"at::vec::convert<{dst_cpp_type}>({src})"
             else:
                 expr = f"at::vec::convert<{dst_cpp_type},{dst_num_vectors},{src_cpp_type},{src_num_vectors}>({src})"
         return expr
diff --git a/torch/csrc/inductor/cpp_prefix.h b/torch/csrc/inductor/cpp_prefix.h
index a51bd74496f..edf87e7bb11 100644
--- a/torch/csrc/inductor/cpp_prefix.h
+++ b/torch/csrc/inductor/cpp_prefix.h
@@ -1321,3 +1321,37 @@ class AMXState {
     tile_release();
   }
 };
+
+// Different versions of fp32->fp8 conversion
+#if defined(CPU_CAPABILITY_AVX512) && !defined(_MSC_VER)
+#if defined(__AVX10_2__)
+
+static inline __m128i cvtfp32_fp8e4m3_avx10_2(const __m512& src) {
+  __m256i f16_vec =
+      _mm512_cvt_roundps_ph(src, _MM_FROUND_TO_NEAREST_INT | _MM_FROUND_NO_EXC);
+  return _mm256_cvtph_hf8(_mm256_castsi256_ph(f16_vec));
+}
+
+static inline at::vec::VectorizedN<at::Float8_e4m3fn, 1>
+convert_float32_float8_e4m3fn(const at::vec::VectorizedN<float, 1>& src_n) {
+  at::vec::Vectorized<float> src = src_n[0];
+  __m128i res128 = cvtfp32_fp8e4m3_avx10_2(src);
+  return at::vec::Vectorized<at::Float8_e4m3fn>(_mm512_castsi128_si512(res128));
+}
+
+#else // no __AVX10_2__
+
+static inline at::vec::VectorizedN<at::Float8_e4m3fn, 1>
+convert_float32_float8_e4m3fn(const at::vec::VectorizedN<float, 1>& src_n) {
+  return at::vec::convert<at::Float8_e4m3fn, 1, float, 1>(src_n);
+}
+
+#endif // __AVX10_2__
+#else // defined(CPU_CAPABILITY_AVX512) && !defined(_MSC_VER)
+static inline at::vec::VectorizedN<at::Float8_e4m3fn, 1>
+convert_float32_float8_e4m3fn(const at::vec::VectorizedN<float, 1>& src_n) {
+  TORCH_CHECK(
+      false,
+      "convert_float32_float8_e4m3fn: Unsupported CPU architecture or OS (require >= AVX512 & Linux)");
+}
+#endif // defined(CPU_CAPABILITY_AVX512) && !defined(_MSC_VER)
Pros:

Aten code is intact

Cons:

More changes of code
A dedicated codegen path for fp32->fp8 conversion

Note:

The code in Inductor CPP backend with newer ISA, such as AMX, won't be tested in CI, which is not brought by this proposal. So, the changes of either option will only be tested by Intel locally, same as before.
Since we are using the new ISA in Inductor CPP backend, users don't have to build PyTorch from source with either option. They only need to run torch.compile with compatible GCC and hardware.

Comments are welcome.
